# Observable State

FuryStack uses observables for reactive state — no React hooks, no external state libraries. Reactive state lives in `ObservableValue` instances exposed by services.

## ObservableValue in services

```typescript
class SessionServiceImpl implements Disposable {
  public currentUser = new ObservableValue<User | null>(null)
  public isAuthenticated = new ObservableValue(false)
  public error = new ObservableValue<string>('')

  constructor(private readonly authApi: AuthApiClient) {}

  public async login(email: string, password: string) {
    try {
      const user = await this.authApi.login({ email, password })
      this.currentUser.setValue(user)
    } catch (err) {
      this.currentUser.setValue(null)
      throw err
    }
  }

  public [Symbol.dispose](): void {
    this.currentUser[Symbol.dispose]()
    this.isAuthenticated[Symbol.dispose]()
    this.error[Symbol.dispose]()
  }
}

export type SessionService = SessionServiceImpl

export const SessionService: Token<SessionService, 'singleton'> = defineService({
  name: 'pi-rat/SessionService',
  lifetime: 'singleton',
  factory: ({ inject, onDispose }) => {
    const impl = new SessionServiceImpl(inject(AuthApiClient))
    // eslint-disable-next-line furystack/prefer-using-wrapper -- Disposal deferred to injector tear-down
    onDispose(() => impl[Symbol.dispose]())
    return impl
  },
})
```

Always type the generic explicitly for nullable or union states (`ObservableValue<User | null>`) — inference widens to `ObservableValue<null>`. The `Impl` class pattern + `defineService` token wrapper is the canonical pi-rat shape (see `CODE_STYLE.mdc`).

## useObservable in components

```typescript
const UserDisplay = Shade({
  customElementName: 'user-display',
  render: ({ injector, useObservable }) => {
    const session = injector.get(SessionService)
    const [user] = useObservable('user', session.currentUser)

    if (!user) return <div>Not logged in</div>
    return <div>{user.name}</div>
  },
})
```

## CRITICAL — pair `useDisposable` with `useObservable` for reactive local state

`.getValue()` reads do **not** subscribe. The component will not re-render when the value changes. Always pair `useDisposable` (creates the observable) with `useObservable` (subscribes to it).

```typescript
// ✅ component re-renders when value changes
const FormComponent = Shade({
  customElementName: 'form-component',
  render: ({ useDisposable, useObservable }) => {
    const isLoadingObs = useDisposable('isLoading', () => new ObservableValue(false))
    const errorObs = useDisposable('error', () => new ObservableValue<string>(''))

    const [isLoading] = useObservable('isLoadingValue', isLoadingObs)
    const [error] = useObservable('errorValue', errorObs)

    const handleSubmit = async () => {
      isLoadingObs.setValue(true)
      try { await doSomething() }
      catch (err) { errorObs.setValue(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'Failed') }
      finally { isLoadingObs.setValue(false) }
    }

    return (
      <div>
        {error && <div style={{ color: 'red' }}>{error}</div>}
        <button disabled={isLoading} onclick={() => void handleSubmit()}>
          {isLoading ? 'Loading...' : 'Submit'}
        </button>
      </div>
    )
  },
})

// ❌ does NOT re-render — getValue() is a snapshot
const Broken = Shade({
  render: ({ useDisposable }) => {
    const isLoading = useDisposable('isLoading', () => new ObservableValue(false))
    return <button disabled={isLoading.getValue()}>{isLoading.getValue() ? '...' : 'Submit'}</button>
  },
})
```

**Rule of thumb:**

- `useDisposable` alone — when you only need to **set** values (event handlers).
- Pair with `useObservable` — when the UI must **react** to value changes.

## Minimize subscriptions — only subscribe to what you need

```typescript
// ✅ only the user, not all users
const UserName = Shade<{ userId: string }>({
  customElementName: 'user-name',
  render: ({ props, injector, useObservable }) => {
    const userService = injector.get(UsersService)
    const [user] = useObservable('user', userService.getUserAsObservable(props.userId))
    return <span>{user?.name ?? 'Loading...'}</span>
  },
})
```

## Service-owned subscriptions

Services that subscribe to other services / event hubs / websockets must clean up. Bind handlers to preserve `this` context, register them in the constructor (or a sync `init()` if startup may be deferred), and dispose them in `[Symbol.dispose]()`. Wire disposal through the factory's `onDispose` so the injector tears it down.

```typescript
class LoggingServiceImpl implements Disposable {
  private onMessage = ((messageData: WebsocketMessage) => {
    if (messageData.type === 'log-entry-added') {
      this.logEntryCache.setExplicitValue({ /* ... */ })
    }
  }).bind(this)

  constructor(private readonly notifications: WebsocketNotificationsService) {
    this.notifications.addListener('onMessage', this.onMessage)
  }

  public [Symbol.dispose](): void {
    this.notifications.removeListener('onMessage', this.onMessage)
  }
}

export type LoggingService = LoggingServiceImpl

export const LoggingService: Token<LoggingService, 'singleton'> = defineService({
  name: 'pi-rat/LoggingService',
  lifetime: 'singleton',
  factory: ({ inject, onDispose }) => {
    const impl = new LoggingServiceImpl(inject(WebsocketNotificationsService))
    // eslint-disable-next-line furystack/prefer-using-wrapper -- Disposal deferred to injector tear-down
    onDispose(() => impl[Symbol.dispose]())
    return impl
  },
})
```

For singleton concurrency-safe init (race-free, fire-and-forget async init, dispose-before-reinit), see `ASYNC_PATTERNS.mdc`.

## Operation wrappers for loading state

```typescript
class DataServiceImpl implements Disposable {
  public isLoading = new ObservableValue(false)
  public data = new ObservableValue<Data | null>(null)

  private operation() {
    this.isLoading.setValue(true)
    return { [Symbol.dispose]: () => this.isLoading.setValue(false) }
  }

  public async loadData() {
    await usingAsync(this.operation(), async () => {
      const result = await this.fetchData()
      this.data.setValue(result)
    })
  }

  public [Symbol.dispose](): void {
    this.isLoading[Symbol.dispose]()
    this.data[Symbol.dispose]()
  }
}
```

## Loading / error / success pattern

```typescript
public async loadUsers() {
  this.isLoading.setValue(true)
  this.error.setValue('')
  try {
    const { result } = await this.apiClient.call({ method: 'GET', action: '/users', query: {} })
    this.users.setValue(result)
  } catch (err) {
    this.error.setValue(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'Failed to load users')
    this.users.setValue([])
  } finally {
    this.isLoading.setValue(false)
  }
}
```

## Performance — debounce user input

Debounce input that triggers expensive work (search, autosave). Typical delays: 300ms search, 500ms autosave.

```typescript
const SearchInput = Shade({
  customElementName: 'search-input',
  render: ({ injector, useDisposable }) => {
    const searchService = injector.get(SearchService)
    const searchTerm = useDisposable('searchTerm', () => new ObservableValue(''))
    let debounceTimer: number | undefined

    const handleInput = (ev: Event) => {
      const value = (ev.target as HTMLInputElement).value
      searchTerm.setValue(value)
      if (debounceTimer) clearTimeout(debounceTimer)
      debounceTimer = window.setTimeout(() => void searchService.search(value), 300)
    }

    return <input type="search" oninput={handleInput} />
  },
})
```

For larger codebases, factor the debounce into a reusable utility.

## Performance — lazy load routes and heavy libs

```typescript
// route lazy-loaded via PiRatLazyLoad — see FRONTEND_PATTERNS.mdc

// heavy lib loaded on demand
class ChartServiceImpl {
  private chartLib?: typeof import('chart.js')

  public async createChart(data: ChartData) {
    if (!this.chartLib) this.chartLib = await import('chart.js')
    return new this.chartLib.Chart(data)
  }
}

export type ChartService = ChartServiceImpl

export const ChartService: Token<ChartService, 'singleton'> = defineService({
  name: 'pi-rat/ChartService',
  lifetime: 'singleton',
  factory: () => new ChartServiceImpl(),
})
```

## Performance — preload critical data in parallel

```typescript
class AppInitImpl {
  constructor(
    private readonly userService: UserService,
    private readonly configService: ConfigService,
  ) {}

  public async initialize() {
    await Promise.all([this.userService.loadCurrentUser(), this.configService.loadConfig()])
  }
}

export type AppInit = AppInitImpl

export const AppInit: Token<AppInit, 'singleton'> = defineService({
  name: 'pi-rat/AppInit',
  lifetime: 'singleton',
  factory: ({ inject }) => new AppInitImpl(inject(UserService), inject(ConfigService)),
})
```

## Memory leak — manual `.subscribe` without dispose

```typescript
// ❌ leak — handler never removed
constructor(private readonly eventService: EventService) {
  this.eventService.addListener('event', (data) => this.handleEvent(data))
}

// ✅ properly managed — register in constructor, remove in dispose
private handleEvent = ((data: EventData) => { /* ... */ }).bind(this)

constructor(private readonly eventService: EventService) {
  this.eventService.addListener('event', this.handleEvent)
}

public [Symbol.dispose](): void {
  this.eventService.removeListener('event', this.handleEvent)
}
```

The `defineService` factory wires `[Symbol.dispose]()` through `onDispose`, so cleanup runs when the injector tears down.

In components, prefer `useObservable` / `useDisposable` for automatic cleanup over manual `.subscribe(...)`.
